 ## Operating characteristics
 
 **Time and Memory Complexity:**
-*   **Memory:** Standard 3DGS memory usage scales linearly with the number of Gaussians. Large scenes can exceed 1 GB [12943-data-compression-and-storage-optimization.md]. Half-precision fetches reduce register pressure and memory bandwidth usage [S1].
-*   **Time:** Rendering is real-time capable due to tile-based rasterization. Optimization time depends on the number of Gaussians and iterations.
+Standard 3DGS training is memory-intensive due to per-Gaussian attributes (position, covariance, opacity, SH coefficients). Large scenes can exceed 1 GB [12943-data-compression-and-storage-optimization.md]. Implementations like `gsplat` use half-precision (FP16) to reduce register pressure and memory bandwidth usage [S1].
 
-**Measured Performance:**
-*   In urban view synthesis (KITTI-360, Waymo), implementations using `gsplat` and sparse convolutions (e.g., EVolSplat) achieve high PSNR/SSIM with low memory consumption (e.g., 10.41 GB for Waymo) and high FPS (83.81 FPS) [S2].
+**Performance:**
+*   **Hardware:** Training and evaluation often occur on high-end GPUs (e.g., NVIDIA V100, RTX4090) [S2][S9].
+*   **Speed:** 3DGS offers real-time rendering speeds (e.g., 81-83 FPS on KITTI-360/Waymo datasets) compared to slower NeRF-based methods [S2].
+*   **Accuracy:** Methods like EVolSplat achieve PSNR of 23.26 dB (KITTI-360) and 23.43 dB (Waymo) with low memory usage (10.41 GB) [S2].
 
 **Strengths and Limitations:**
-*   **Strengths:** Real-time rendering, high fidelity, differentiable optimization.
-*   **Limitations:** High memory footprint for large scenes, sensitivity to camera distribution for scene extent, artifacts in sparse views or noisy data without variants like Denoise-GS [S5].
-
-**When to Use:**
-*   Use standard implementation for dense-view, well-lit scenes.
-*   Use variants (Corrected Scene-Extent, Denoise-GS, Underwater) for specific challenges like clustered cameras, sparse/noisy views, or aquatic environments.
+*   **Strengths:** Real-time rendering, high fidelity, explicit representation allows for easy editing and mesh extraction.
+*   **Limitations:** High memory footprint, sensitivity to camera distribution (addressed by Corrected Scene Extent), struggles with specular highlights (addressed by View-Dependent Opacity), and poor performance in sparse/noisy views (addressed by Denoise-GS) or underwater environments (addressed by Backscatter Estimation) [S3][S4][S5][S6, unofficial][S7].
 
 ## Background
 
-3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced as a real-time explicit scene representation method [26]. The implementation details described here are based on the `gsplat` library [S2] and various extensions addressing specific limitations such as scene extent bias [S3], defocus blur [S4], noise [S5], specular highlights [S6, unofficial], underwater scattering [S7], large-scale partitioning [S8], and inverse rendering [S9]. The use of half-precision kernels and specific loss scaling strategies [S1] are critical for stable training on modern GPUs.
+3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced as a method for real-time radiance field rendering using explicit 3D Gaussian primitives. It has since evolved through various optimizations and extensions to handle specific challenges such as large-scale scenes, inverse rendering, and non-standard environments. The implementation details described here stem from various research efforts aiming to improve stability, accuracy, and efficiency of the core 3DGS framework.
 
